// GroupQueryAttention (com.microsoft) — f16, PACKED QKV, DECODE ONLY, KV-PARALLEL.
//
// This module is a second implementation of the *same* arithmetic `gqa_f16.comp` performs, for
// the one shape where that kernel's dispatch geometry cannot use the machine: `seq_len == 1`.
// `gqa_f16` gives one invocation to each `(batch, head, query position)`, so a decode step is
// `B * Nq` invocations — 32 on Phi-3.5 — and each of them walks the whole KV cache serially.
// At `past = 1024` that kernel holds 93.5% of GPU time (docs/PERF.md §26.4/§26.7) and there is
// no packing left to do: the invocation count is a property of the model, not of the geometry.
//
// The lever §26.7 names is parallelism over the **KV sequence** inside a workgroup. That is what
// this module is: one workgroup per `(batch, query head)`, `W` lanes striding the cache, each
// lane running the same online softmax over its own subset, and a halving-tree merge in shared
// memory that combines the per-lane partials into the one answer.
//
// # `gqa_f16.comp` is not modified, and that is a requirement rather than a convenience
//
// Prefill, the fallback, and every graph this module refuses keep running the byte-identical
// SPIR-V they ran before. A decode-only rewrite of the shipping kernel would have put prefill's
// correctness — including the sibling-key recompute that fixed a real data race — behind a
// change whose evidence is entirely about decode. Two modules, one selector, no shared risk.
//
// # Portability: Vulkan 1.1 core, and nothing else
//
//   * **No subgroup operations, no wave intrinsics, no extensions beyond the 16-bit storage one
//     `gqa_f16` already requires, no optional features, no device queries.** The reduction is
//     `shared` memory plus `barrier()`, which is core compute functionality in every Vulkan 1.1
//     implementation. A subgroup reduction would be faster on the devices that have the right
//     subgroup size and would silently not exist on the ones that do not.
//   * **The shared footprint is a constant, checkable by inspection: 8,320 B.**
//     `s_max[16] + s_sum[16]` is 128 B and `s_acc[16 * 128]` is 8,192 B. Vulkan 1.1 guarantees
//     `maxComputeSharedMemorySize >= 16384`, so this module fits on every conformant
//     implementation with 49.2% of the guaranteed floor to spare — and it fits *as a module*,
//     not as one specialisation of it. Sizing the arrays by the lane-count specialisation
//     constant was considered and declined for exactly that reason: it would make the memory
//     bound a property of each pipeline, so "does this module fit the floor" would stop being a
//     question anyone can answer by reading the file.
//   * **The workgroup size is at most 16 invocations**, well inside the `>= 128`
//     `maxComputeWorkGroupInvocations` the specification guarantees.
//   * **Every `barrier()` is in uniform control flow.** They sit at the top of a loop whose
//     bound (`active_lanes`) is a function of `pc` and of `seqlens_k[b]` only — `b` is fixed for the
//     whole workgroup — so every invocation of the workgroup executes the same number of them.
//
// # Effective lanes: the KV arena's capacity ambiguity, answered in the shader
//
// The host chooses `W` (specialisation constant 0) from the *declared* cache extent. Under the
// KV arena that extent is a **capacity**, not a length: `past_key.shape[2]` is how much room the
// caller reserved and `seqlens_k` is how much of it is real. A lane count picked from a capacity
// would give a 4,096-slot arena holding 8 tokens the full 16 lanes and a 4-deep merge tree over
// 8 positions, which is slower than not parallelising at all.
//
// So `W` is treated as an **upper bound** and the number of lanes that actually participate is
// recomputed here, from `seqlens_k[b]`, by the same rule the host uses. `active_lanes` is uniform
// across the workgroup (it depends only on `b`), it never exceeds `W`, and at `active_lanes == 1` this
// module degenerates to exactly `gqa_f16`'s serial walk with no barrier executed at all. That
// also covers ragged batches, where one sequence in the batch is far shorter than another.
//
// # Non-finite values
//
// Stated rather than implied: this module does **not** clamp, sanitise, or scrub. A NaN score
// fails `sc > max_s`, `exp(NaN - max_s)` is NaN, and the NaN reaches the output — which is what
// `gqa_f16` does, term for term. The merge is ordinary arithmetic on the lanes' partials, so a
// NaN in any lane propagates through `s_sum`/`s_acc` to the same place. `-inf` scores contribute
// `exp(-inf - max) == 0` and are dropped, again as in `gqa_f16`. The one behaviour that is *not*
// shared is bit-exactness: summing a fixed set of terms in a different order is a different
// rounding, so equivalence against `gqa_f16` is asserted within a declared tolerance and never
// as bit-equality. See docs/DESIGN.md §8.14.
//
// # Layout — identical to `gqa_f16.comp`, deliberately
//
// Same 9 bindings in the same order, same 36-byte push block, same f16 two-per-`uint` packing,
// same atomic half-word writes, same `kv_write_leader`/`copy_leader` predicates. The host builds
// one push-constant block and one binding list for both modules, so a divergence here would be a
// silent mis-binding rather than a compile error.
//
//   binding 0: packed_qkv    readonly  [B, S, (Nq + 2*Nkv) * D]      f16 packed as uint
//   binding 1: past_key      readonly  [B, Nkv, past_stride, D]      f16 packed as uint
//   binding 2: past_value    readonly  [B, Nkv, past_stride, D]      f16 packed as uint
//   binding 3: seqlens_k     readonly  [B]                           int32
//   binding 4: cos_cache     readonly  [max_seq, rotary_dim/2]       f16 packed as uint
//   binding 5: sin_cache     readonly  [max_seq, rotary_dim/2]       f16 packed as uint
//   binding 6: attn_output   readwrite [B, S, Nq * D]                f16 packed as uint
//   binding 7: present_key   readwrite [B, Nkv, present_len, D]      f16 packed as uint
//   binding 8: present_value readwrite [B, Nkv, present_len, D]      f16 packed as uint

#version 450
#extension GL_EXT_shader_16bit_storage : require

// Specialisation id 0 — the host's `gqa_decode_kv_lanes`, the number of lanes this pipeline was
// built for. Defaults to 1, which is the serial walk: a pipeline built without the constant is
// arithmetically `gqa_f16` and is never dispatched, because a lane count of 1 is the host's
// refusal value.
layout(local_size_x_id = 0, local_size_x = 1, local_size_y = 1, local_size_z = 1) in;

// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Portability bounds. Both are plain constants, not specialisation constants:
// see the shared-footprint note in the header.
//
// MIN_KV_PER_LANE is duplicated from `GQA_DECODE_MIN_KV_PER_LANE` in
// `rust/src/ops/attention.rs` and pinned to it by a `cargo test --lib` test that reads this
// file, so the host's lane ladder and this one cannot drift apart silently.
// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
const uint MAX_KV_LANES     = 16u;
const uint MAX_HEAD_DIM     = 128u;
const uint MIN_KV_PER_LANE  = 32u;

// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Push constants — byte-for-byte the block `gqa_f16.comp` declares.
// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
layout(push_constant) uniform PC {
    uint  batch_size;
    uint  seq_len;
    uint  num_heads;
    uint  kv_num_heads;
    uint  head_dim;
    uint  rotary_dim;
    uint  present_len;   // KV *write* stride — the present buffer's S dimension
    uint  past_stride;   // the past buffer's S dimension
    float scale;
} pc;

// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Buffers (f16 elements packed two-per-uint)
// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
layout(set = 0, binding = 0, std430) readonly  buffer BufQKV  { uint d[]; } qkv;
layout(set = 0, binding = 1, std430) readonly  buffer BufPK   { uint d[]; } past_k;
layout(set = 0, binding = 2, std430) readonly  buffer BufPV   { uint d[]; } past_v;
layout(set = 0, binding = 3, std430) readonly  buffer BufSeq  { int  d[]; } seqlens;
layout(set = 0, binding = 4, std430) readonly  buffer BufCos  { uint d[]; } cos_buf;
layout(set = 0, binding = 5, std430) readonly  buffer BufSin  { uint d[]; } sin_buf;
layout(set = 0, binding = 6, std430)           buffer BufOut  { uint d[]; } attn_out;
layout(set = 0, binding = 7, std430)           buffer BufPresK { uint d[]; } pres_k;
layout(set = 0, binding = 8, std430)           buffer BufPresV { uint d[]; } pres_v;

// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The reduction workspace. 8,320 B, constant for every pipeline built from this module.
//
// `s_acc` is flat rather than 2-D so that the stride is stated once, here, and every index is
// `lane * MAX_HEAD_DIM + d`.
// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
shared float s_max[MAX_KV_LANES];
shared float s_sum[MAX_KV_LANES];
shared float s_acc[MAX_KV_LANES * MAX_HEAD_DIM];

// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Scalar f16 read helpers (one per readonly buffer — GLSL cannot parameterise
// buffer blocks, so each buffer gets its own helper)
// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
float f16_qkv(uint i) {
    vec2 p = unpackHalf2x16(qkv.d[i >> 1u]);
    return (i & 1u) == 0u ? p.x : p.y;
}
float f16_pk(uint i) {
    vec2 p = unpackHalf2x16(past_k.d[i >> 1u]);
    return (i & 1u) == 0u ? p.x : p.y;
}
float f16_pv(uint i) {
    vec2 p = unpackHalf2x16(past_v.d[i >> 1u]);
    return (i & 1u) == 0u ? p.x : p.y;
}
float f16_cos(uint i) {
    vec2 p = unpackHalf2x16(cos_buf.d[i >> 1u]);
    return (i & 1u) == 0u ? p.x : p.y;
}
float f16_sin(uint i) {
    vec2 p = unpackHalf2x16(sin_buf.d[i >> 1u]);
    return (i & 1u) == 0u ? p.x : p.y;
}

// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Scalar f16 write helpers (atomic to protect the neighbour half-word)
// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
void wr_out(uint i, float v) {
    uint bits = packHalf2x16(vec2(v, v));
    if ((i & 1u) == 0u) {
        atomicAnd(attn_out.d[i >> 1u], 0xFFFF0000u);
        atomicOr (attn_out.d[i >> 1u], bits & 0x0000FFFFu);
    } else {
        atomicAnd(attn_out.d[i >> 1u], 0x0000FFFFu);
        atomicOr (attn_out.d[i >> 1u], bits & 0xFFFF0000u);
    }
}
void wr_presk(uint i, float v) {
    uint bits = packHalf2x16(vec2(v, v));
    if ((i & 1u) == 0u) {
        atomicAnd(pres_k.d[i >> 1u], 0xFFFF0000u);
        atomicOr (pres_k.d[i >> 1u], bits & 0x0000FFFFu);
    } else {
        atomicAnd(pres_k.d[i >> 1u], 0x0000FFFFu);
        atomicOr (pres_k.d[i >> 1u], bits & 0xFFFF0000u);
    }
}
void wr_presv(uint i, float v) {
    uint bits = packHalf2x16(vec2(v, v));
    if ((i & 1u) == 0u) {
        atomicAnd(pres_v.d[i >> 1u], 0xFFFF0000u);
        atomicOr (pres_v.d[i >> 1u], bits & 0x0000FFFFu);
    } else {
        atomicAnd(pres_v.d[i >> 1u], 0x0000FFFFu);
        atomicOr (pres_v.d[i >> 1u], bits & 0xFFFF0000u);
    }
}

// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Main
// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
void main() {
    uint S   = pc.seq_len;
    uint Nq  = pc.num_heads;
    uint Nkv = pc.kv_num_heads;
    uint D   = pc.head_dim;
    uint rh  = pc.rotary_dim >> 1u;   // cos/sin entries per position = rotary_dim/2

    uint lane = gl_LocalInvocationID.x;
    // The lane budget this pipeline was built with. `min` is belt and braces: the host clamps to
    // `GQA_DECODE_MAX_KV_LANES` and a `cargo test --lib` test holds it there, but a workgroup
    // wider than the shared arrays must degrade to fewer lanes rather than index past them.
    uint budget = min(gl_WorkGroupSize.x, MAX_KV_LANES);

    // One workgroup per (batch b, query head h). Decode only: S == 1, so there is no query
    // position to decode out of the workgroup id and `s_local` is 0 everywhere below.
    uint wg = gl_WorkGroupID.x;
    uint b  = wg / Nq;
    uint h  = wg % Nq;
    // FAIL CLOSED. Uniform for the whole workgroup — `wg` is the same for every invocation — so
    // returning here cannot leave some invocations waiting at a barrier the others skipped.
    if (b >= pc.batch_size) return;
    // Decode only, by construction and by the host selector. Refusing here as well keeps the
    // module correct on its own terms rather than only in combination with its caller: at
    // `S > 1` the `past_len <= t < tok_pos` sibling-key case exists and this module has no
    // branch for it. Also uniform.
    if (S != 1u) return;

    uint kv_h = h / (Nq / Nkv);        // KV head (group_size = Nq/Nkv; MHA has group_size = 1)
    // ORT defines `seqlens_k[b]` as total_sequence_length - 1, and total = past + seq_len.
    // At S == 1 that makes `past_len == seqlens_k[b]`, but the general expression is kept so
    // this line reads the same as `gqa_f16.comp`'s and cannot drift from it.
    int  seqlens_b = seqlens.d[int(b)];
    uint past_len  = uint(max(seqlens_b + 1 - int(S), 0));
    uint tok_pos   = past_len;         // absolute position of the single query token
    uint total_len = tok_pos + 1u;     // causal: [0 .. tok_pos] inclusive

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Effective lane count — the same ladder `gqa_decode_kv_lanes_with` runs on the host, over
    // the length this dispatch actually has rather than the extent the buffer was declared
    // with. Uniform across the workgroup: `total_len` depends only on `b`, and `budget` is a
    // specialisation constant.
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    uint active_lanes = 1u;
    while (active_lanes * 2u <= budget && (total_len / (active_lanes * 2u)) >= MIN_KV_PER_LANE) {
        active_lanes *= 2u;
    }

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    // 1. Load Q, K, V from packed_qkv. Every lane loads the same values: they are functions of
    //    (b, h, kv_h) only, the loop is D <= 128 iterations, and the alternative — one lane
    //    loading and a barrier to publish — costs a barrier to save a read that is already in
    //    cache for every lane after the first.
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    uint qkv_row = b * (Nq + 2u * Nkv) * D;   // S == 1, so s_local == 0
    uint q_base  = qkv_row + h            * D;
    uint k_base  = qkv_row + Nq           * D + kv_h * D;
    uint v_base  = qkv_row + (Nq + Nkv)   * D + kv_h * D;

    float q[128], k_new[128], v_new[128], acc[128];
    for (uint d = 0u; d < D; d++) {
        q[d]     = f16_qkv(q_base + d);
        k_new[d] = f16_qkv(k_base + d);
        v_new[d] = f16_qkv(v_base + d);
        acc[d]   = 0.0;
    }

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    // 2. RoPE on Q and K (neox / half-rotation: first rh dims are "x", next rh are "y").
    //    Identical arithmetic to `gqa_f16.comp` step 2.
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    {
        uint cos_row = tok_pos * rh;
        for (uint ri = 0u; ri < rh; ri++) {
            float c = f16_cos(cos_row + ri);
            float s = f16_sin(cos_row + ri);

            float qx = q[ri];        float qy = q[ri + rh];
            q[ri]      = qx * c - qy * s;
            q[ri + rh] = qy * c + qx * s;

            float kx = k_new[ri];    float ky = k_new[ri + rh];
            k_new[ri]      = kx * c - ky * s;
            k_new[ri + rh] = ky * c + kx * s;
        }
    }

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    // 3. Write the rotated K/V into present at tok_pos.
    //
    //    Lane 0 only. In `gqa_f16` the writer is the invocation that owns `(b, h, s_local)`;
    //    here the workgroup owns `(b, h)` and any single lane of it will do, so the choice is
    //    lane 0 rather than "whichever lane's stride happens to land on tok_pos". The
    //    `kv_write_leader` predicate and the capacity guard are `gqa_f16`'s, unchanged: all
    //    G query heads of a KV group name the same words, so exactly one of them writes.
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    uint group_size      = Nq / Nkv;
    bool kv_write_leader = (h % group_size) == 0u;
    if (lane == 0u && kv_write_leader && tok_pos < pc.present_len) {
        uint pb = (b * Nkv + kv_h) * pc.present_len * D + tok_pos * D;
        for (uint d = 0u; d < D; d++) {
            wr_presk(pb + d, k_new[d]);
            wr_presv(pb + d, v_new[d]);
        }
    }

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    // 3a. Growing cache: the past tokens must appear in `present`, and the copy is fused into
    //     the walk below exactly as it is in `gqa_f16` — the element is loaded once and used
    //     twice. What changes is who copies what: the lanes partition `[0, past_len)` between
    //     them, so each past position is copied by exactly one lane and no position is missed.
    //     Under the arena convention `present_len == past_stride`, `copy_leader` is false, and
    //     the copy compiles out of the run.
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    bool copy_leader = pc.present_len != pc.past_stride && kv_write_leader;
    uint copy_dst    = (b * Nkv + kv_h) * pc.present_len * D;

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    // 4. Per-lane online softmax over a strided subset of the cache.
    //
    //    Lane `l` walks t = l, l + active_lanes, l + 2*active_lanes, ... so the lanes partition
    //    [0, total_len) exactly. The recurrence is `gqa_f16.comp` step 4's, unchanged; the only
    //    difference is which values of `t` this invocation sees.
    //
    //    Lanes at or above `active_lanes` do not walk. They are not merged either, so walking would
    //    only duplicate `copy_leader`'s writes.
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    float max_s = -1.0e30;
    float sum_e = 0.0;

    if (lane < active_lanes) {
        for (uint t = lane; t < total_len; t += active_lanes) {
            float dot = 0.0;
            uint kv_base;
            if (t < past_len) {
                kv_base = (b * Nkv + kv_h) * pc.past_stride * D + t * D;
                if (copy_leader) {
                    for (uint d = 0u; d < D; d++) {
                        float kv = f16_pk(kv_base + d);
                        dot += q[d] * kv;
                        wr_presk(copy_dst + t * D + d, kv);
                    }
                } else {
                    for (uint d = 0u; d < D; d++) dot += q[d] * f16_pk(kv_base + d);
                }
            } else {
                // t == tok_pos — the self key. `gqa_f16`'s third case
                // (`past_len <= t < tok_pos`, a sibling query position) cannot arise here:
                // it is non-empty only when S > 1, which this module returned on above.
                for (uint d = 0u; d < D; d++) dot += q[d] * k_new[d];
            }
            float sc = dot * pc.scale;

            if (sc > max_s) {
                float factor = exp(max_s - sc);
                for (uint d = 0u; d < D; d++) acc[d] *= factor;
                sum_e *= factor;
                max_s = sc;
            }
            float e = exp(sc - max_s);
            sum_e += e;

            if (t < past_len) {
                kv_base = (b * Nkv + kv_h) * pc.past_stride * D + t * D;
                if (copy_leader) {
                    for (uint d = 0u; d < D; d++) {
                        float vv = f16_pv(kv_base + d);
                        acc[d] += e * vv;
                        wr_presv(copy_dst + t * D + d, vv);
                    }
                } else {
                    for (uint d = 0u; d < D; d++) acc[d] += e * f16_pv(kv_base + d);
                }
            } else {
                for (uint d = 0u; d < D; d++) acc[d] += e * v_new[d];
            }
        }
    }

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    // 5. Publish the partials and merge them.
    //
    //    A lane that walked nothing publishes the identity element of the merge — max
    //    -1.0e30, sum 0, acc 0 — which is what `max_s`/`sum_e`/`acc` already hold, so the
    //    empty case needs no special path.
    //
    //    The merge of two online-softmax partials (m_a, s_a, acc_a) and (m_b, s_b, acc_b):
    //
    //        m  = max(m_a, m_b)
    //        s  = s_a * exp(m_a - m) + s_b * exp(m_b - m)
    //        acc = acc_a * exp(m_a - m) + acc_b * exp(m_b - m)
    //
    //    which is the same rescale-to-the-joint-maximum step the serial recurrence performs
    //    every time it meets a larger score. Both factors are `exp` of a non-positive number,
    //    so neither can overflow, and an empty partial's factor is `exp(-1.0e30 - m) == 0`
    //    against any finite `m`.
    //
    //    Lanes at or above MAX_KV_LANES publish nothing: `active_lanes <= budget <= MAX_KV_LANES`,
    //    so no such lane is ever read.
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    if (lane < MAX_KV_LANES) {
        s_max[lane] = max_s;
        s_sum[lane] = sum_e;
        for (uint d = 0u; d < D; d++) s_acc[lane * MAX_HEAD_DIM + d] = acc[d];
    }

    // The loop bound is uniform, so every invocation of the workgroup executes the same number
    // of barriers. At `active_lanes == 1` the loop body never runs and no barrier is executed at all,
    // which is the degenerate case this module shares with `gqa_f16`.
    for (uint stride = 1u; stride < active_lanes; stride <<= 1u) {
        memoryBarrierShared();
        barrier();
        uint partner = lane + stride;
        if ((lane & ((stride << 1u) - 1u)) == 0u && partner < active_lanes) {
            float ma = s_max[lane];
            float mb = s_max[partner];
            float mn = max(ma, mb);
            float fa = exp(ma - mn);
            float fb = exp(mb - mn);
            s_max[lane] = mn;
            s_sum[lane] = s_sum[lane] * fa + s_sum[partner] * fb;
            for (uint d = 0u; d < D; d++) {
                s_acc[lane * MAX_HEAD_DIM + d] =
                    s_acc[lane * MAX_HEAD_DIM + d] * fa + s_acc[partner * MAX_HEAD_DIM + d] * fb;
            }
        }
    }

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    // 6. Normalise and write attn_output[b, 0, h*D .. (h+1)*D - 1].
    //    output layout: [B, S, Nq * D] with S == 1.
    //
    //    Lane 0 wrote slot 0 itself on the last merge iteration, so no further barrier is
    //    needed before reading it back.
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    if (lane == 0u) {
        float inv_s = 1.0 / s_sum[0];
        uint  ob    = b * Nq * D + h * D;
        for (uint d = 0u; d < D; d++) wr_out(ob + d, s_acc[d] * inv_s);
    }
}
